WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.255 --> 00:00:13.072
Ben Nigh: What do "The
Exorcist," "Stand by Me,"

00:00:13.072 --> 00:00:16.030
"Blockers," and "Big Little
Lies" have in common?

00:00:16.030 --> 00:00:17.108
A vomit scene.

00:00:17.108 --> 00:00:19.530
For every disgusting
vomit scene in a movie,

00:00:19.530 --> 00:00:22.640
there's an effects team that
actually had to make the stuff,

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:24.890
and an actor who had to sell it.

00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:26.590
Hi, everyone. My name is Ben.

00:00:26.590 --> 00:00:30.930
Today, I'm heading to NYSPFX
in Red Hook, Brooklyn,

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:33.900
and I'm gonna see how fake
puke is made for movies and TV.

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:34.920
And I couldn't be more excited,

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:37.736
because I'm gonna get to
try some of it myself.

00:00:37.736 --> 00:00:39.987
Grab a snack.

00:00:39.987 --> 00:00:42.761
Mmm, you might not want to
eat while you watch this.

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:49.530
This is Mike Myers, a
special effects artist

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:52.480
and the founder of NYSPFX.

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:54.810
Myers and his team created
some of the coolest

00:00:54.810 --> 00:00:57.630
practical effects for the
Batman prequel show "Gotham"

00:00:57.630 --> 00:01:00.833
and can do everything from
breakaway glass to fake blood.

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:03.900
But today is all about the puke,

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:05.850
which Myers executed flawlessly

00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:08.936
in the 2015 film "While We're Young."

00:01:08.936 --> 00:01:10.251
(man vomits)

00:01:10.251 --> 00:01:12.712
(woman vomits)

00:01:12.712 --> 00:01:14.660
(woman vomits)

00:01:14.660 --> 00:01:15.930
Watch the carpet.

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:19.300
Ben: Fake vomit comes in all
shapes, sizes, and textures.

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:21.880
It can be made to look
realistic or exaggerated,

00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:23.720
depending on the scene and genre.

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:25.790
In "While We're Young,"
they used a combination

00:01:25.790 --> 00:01:28.250
of potato-leek and split-pea soup.

00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:31.940
Mike Myers: We're
attempting today Top Ramen

00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:35.123
to give that squiggly, soft noodle

00:01:35.123 --> 00:01:37.783
projectile nastiness

00:01:37.783 --> 00:01:40.343
to hopefully stick to someone's
lip when they're done.

00:01:40.343 --> 00:01:43.650
Today, we have some frozen
berries that we're gonna mix up.

00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:45.734
SFX artist: All right, so
here comes the tomato paste.

00:01:45.734 --> 00:01:46.569
Ben: Yum.

00:01:46.569 --> 00:01:48.700
SFX artist: A little
thickener, little color.

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:50.280
Ben: So, we have tomato paste.

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:52.730
That just splashed all over me, perfect.

00:01:52.730 --> 00:01:53.563
Brian Walsh: You're in the splash zone.

00:01:53.563 --> 00:01:54.563
Ben: Yeah.

00:01:56.410 --> 00:01:58.060
Look how good that looks.

00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:03.700
This looks very familiar to
me. But not too familiar.

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:04.710
For this experiment,

00:02:04.710 --> 00:02:06.670
we used three different types of puke.

00:02:06.670 --> 00:02:09.970
So, we got our first
fake puke right there.

00:02:09.970 --> 00:02:11.110
That's peas and water.

00:02:11.110 --> 00:02:12.720
And then this was a nice mix

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:15.570
of ramen, water, and tomato paste.

00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:17.743
And so now we've got berries, right.

00:02:19.130 --> 00:02:20.880
Third one is gonna have berries and water.

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:21.890
Brian: Just water, yeah.

00:02:21.890 --> 00:02:22.723
Ben: Great.

00:02:22.723 --> 00:02:24.020
This is definitely the kind of fake vomit

00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:26.352
that's gonna stain my clothing.

00:02:26.352 --> 00:02:27.185
Brian: Yes, for sure.

00:02:27.185 --> 00:02:29.682
Ben: But I anticipated
that, so I wore my jeans

00:02:29.682 --> 00:02:32.910
that have pizza on them already, so.

00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:33.910
Once we had the mixtures,

00:02:33.910 --> 00:02:35.710
it was time to load them into the tubes.

00:02:35.710 --> 00:02:38.120
Now, this stage is
actually just as important

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:39.690
to making convincing fake puke

00:02:39.690 --> 00:02:41.750
as the actor who has to sell the scene.

00:02:41.750 --> 00:02:45.780
Mike: The pumps that we
utilize are made for liquids,

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:49.860
not vomit, thick, pasty substances.

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:54.050
We use our main liquid as our propellant

00:02:54.050 --> 00:02:57.760
and then in the vertical
tubes on the pumping

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:00.230
incorporate the thicker
paste that will then

00:03:00.230 --> 00:03:02.550
be pushed upward and outward first.

00:03:02.550 --> 00:03:04.630
Ben: The mixture also
needed to be this texture

00:03:04.630 --> 00:03:06.640
for another reason: realism.

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:08.760
The medium-density,
thick part of the mixture

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:10.540
will be fed into the top of the tubes,

00:03:10.540 --> 00:03:12.880
and the rest that comes out
will be more like liquid,

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:15.790
which Myers says follows the
pattern of real-life vomit.

00:03:15.790 --> 00:03:17.690
Ben: Yeah, that looks really gross.

00:03:19.010 --> 00:03:21.470
I needed a little acting advice first.

00:03:21.470 --> 00:03:25.270
Mike: When they have to,
like, regurgitate and project,

00:03:25.270 --> 00:03:28.700
there does have to be
some body motion involved,

00:03:28.700 --> 00:03:30.270
but it pretty much has to say

00:03:30.270 --> 00:03:32.700
if there's my hose and
if I'm regurgitating,

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:35.330
I can't (retches) go
up because then my hose

00:03:35.330 --> 00:03:36.610
is coming out of my mouth.

00:03:36.610 --> 00:03:39.350
So he has to know to only go forward

00:03:39.350 --> 00:03:42.340
and open his mouth wide
and keep his mouth open.

00:03:42.340 --> 00:03:43.798
'Cause if he closes his mouth

00:03:43.798 --> 00:03:45.410
and the vomit's still coming out,

00:03:45.410 --> 00:03:46.670
what a wasted take that is.

00:03:46.670 --> 00:03:49.170
Ben: OK, we're gonna do this whole bucket.

00:03:49.170 --> 00:03:51.500
Mike: Yeah, that's about
8 gallons of worth of...

00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:52.333
SFX artist: Yeah, that's a lot.

00:03:52.333 --> 00:03:53.470
Mike: Chum.

00:03:53.470 --> 00:03:55.601
Ben: This is probably
the chunkiest mixture.

00:03:55.601 --> 00:03:58.612
And there was going to be a lot of it.

00:03:58.612 --> 00:04:00.333
Brian: Three, two, one.

00:04:00.333 --> 00:04:01.166
(retching)

00:04:01.166 --> 00:04:03.336
One, one hundred, two.

00:04:03.336 --> 00:04:05.925
(coughing)

00:04:05.925 --> 00:04:06.758
(groaning)

00:04:06.758 --> 00:04:07.591
Another one.

00:04:07.591 --> 00:04:08.759
Three, two, one.

00:04:08.759 --> 00:04:10.093
(retching)

00:04:10.093 --> 00:04:11.343
One, one hundred, two.

00:04:13.700 --> 00:04:14.790
Ben: I think getting the timing right

00:04:14.790 --> 00:04:16.752
is, like, the first initial challenge.

00:04:16.752 --> 00:04:19.180
You hear it coming up, and so,
like, as long as you're just

00:04:19.180 --> 00:04:22.740
kinda, like, you know, ready
to go, then you're good to go.

00:04:22.740 --> 00:04:25.390
The pea-soup mixture is
different for several reasons.

00:04:25.390 --> 00:04:26.720
It's mixed with a lot of water,

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:28.890
making it a lot more liquidy.

00:04:28.890 --> 00:04:31.180
And for this, they
instead use a pressure pot

00:04:31.180 --> 00:04:32.583
with clear tubes attached.

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:38.800
I'm like the guy in the group
project that doesn't help,

00:04:39.907 --> 00:04:41.690
but still gets an A anyways.

00:04:41.690 --> 00:04:42.523
Great.

00:04:42.523 --> 00:04:44.100
Mike: This hose allows you the flexibility

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:45.470
where you're not rigid.

00:04:45.470 --> 00:04:46.303
Ben: OK, cool.

00:04:46.303 --> 00:04:47.850
Mike: So, because you
have a guy behind you,

00:04:47.850 --> 00:04:50.320
you can go left, right, up, down,

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:52.150
turn to that camera and splash it.

00:04:52.150 --> 00:04:52.983
Ben: Cool.

00:04:54.070 --> 00:04:56.238
Do you want me to puke on you?

00:04:56.238 --> 00:04:58.370
Ben: (laughs) Puke near
you? Puke that way?

00:04:58.370 --> 00:05:00.700
And you said that it's
gonna dribble down my lip?

00:05:00.700 --> 00:05:01.790
Mike: Once we shut it off,

00:05:01.790 --> 00:05:03.290
it'll dribble, dribble, dribble.

00:05:03.290 --> 00:05:04.123
Ben: Great, cool.

00:05:04.123 --> 00:05:05.530
Mike: And that gives some authenticity.

00:05:05.530 --> 00:05:07.830
'Cause it's also got a
deflector in the front

00:05:07.830 --> 00:05:08.717
that's gonna spray

00:05:08.717 --> 00:05:09.960
a little.
Ben: Ooh, sweet.

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:12.010
This works well for a scene involving

00:05:12.010 --> 00:05:13.430
out-of-control vomiting.

00:05:13.430 --> 00:05:14.430
Might wanna watch out, Nick.

00:05:14.430 --> 00:05:16.263
One piece of advice I would give:

00:05:19.050 --> 00:05:20.523
Aim away from the camera.

00:05:22.545 --> 00:05:23.378
(laughing in the background)

00:05:23.378 --> 00:05:27.490
I am covered in fake puke,
and I'm not the only one.

00:05:27.490 --> 00:05:28.760
For some types of puke,

00:05:28.760 --> 00:05:30.727
no rigs or hoses are required.

00:05:34.637 --> 00:05:35.867
Bonnie: Of Facebook and reality...

00:05:35.867 --> 00:05:36.900
(Madeline throws up)

00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:38.159
G------ it!

00:05:38.159 --> 00:05:39.112
Madeline: Oh, my God.

00:05:39.112 --> 00:05:40.100
Bonnie: S---.

00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:40.933
Madeline: It's the shrimp.

00:05:41.810 --> 00:05:44.240
Ben: For a spit take, a
member of the effects team

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:46.120
would scoop a little bit of fake puke up.

00:05:46.120 --> 00:05:47.370
Oop, that seems like a lot.

00:05:47.370 --> 00:05:48.536
And the actor would swallow it

00:05:48.536 --> 00:05:50.127
and then spit it up.

00:05:50.127 --> 00:05:51.540
(spits)

00:05:51.540 --> 00:05:52.373
Like that?

00:05:52.373 --> 00:05:53.606
Brian: Yeah, that's pretty good.

00:05:53.606 --> 00:05:54.772
Ben: How did that look, guys?

00:05:54.772 --> 00:05:55.605
Producer: That looked great.

00:05:55.605 --> 00:05:56.438
Ben: Should I do some more?

00:05:56.438 --> 00:05:57.271
Producer: How'd it taste?

00:05:57.271 --> 00:05:58.104
Ben: Actually, really good.

00:05:58.104 --> 00:05:59.700
There's always room for
a little improvement.

00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:00.870
Are there any tips you can give me

00:06:00.870 --> 00:06:03.170
for how to improve my performance?

00:06:03.170 --> 00:06:04.280
Brian: Well a good thing to do would just

00:06:04.280 --> 00:06:05.530
a little more retching.

00:06:05.530 --> 00:06:07.140
A little shoulder movement,

00:06:07.140 --> 00:06:08.550
a little try to sell it a little more.

00:06:08.550 --> 00:06:10.550
And even if you put your hand in front,

00:06:10.550 --> 00:06:12.250
and get a little bit more,

00:06:12.250 --> 00:06:13.749
you know, separate where it's coming from.

00:06:13.749 --> 00:06:15.440
Maybe a little bit
coming down on yourself.

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:16.640
Ben: All right, no problem.

00:06:18.081 --> 00:06:19.639
(coughing)

00:06:19.639 --> 00:06:21.806
(gushing)

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:26.380
I felt like I was in the most control.

00:06:26.380 --> 00:06:29.176
I could kind of, obviously,
I could time it myself.

00:06:29.176 --> 00:06:32.950
I didn't have to, to
time it up with the rig

00:06:32.950 --> 00:06:34.053
or anything like that,

00:06:35.010 --> 00:06:37.613
which I think allowed me to
get more into my performance.

00:06:37.613 --> 00:06:38.446
(laughs)

00:06:38.446 --> 00:06:40.630
Love berries, so.

00:06:40.630 --> 00:06:42.853
That spit take is the one for me.

00:06:43.780 --> 00:06:47.170
And with no tubes to cover up
nor massive sprays to avoid,

00:06:47.170 --> 00:06:49.610
the camera can look
straight on to the actor.

00:06:49.610 --> 00:06:53.190
However, there are many ways
to hide these pesky tubes.

00:06:53.190 --> 00:06:55.890
Sometimes, they're simply
taken out in post-production.

00:06:55.890 --> 00:06:58.530
Other times, it's all about
where you place the camera.

00:06:58.530 --> 00:07:00.960
And while CGI is used to aid the process,

00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:03.640
fake-vomit scenes are still
largely done practically.

00:07:03.640 --> 00:07:05.608
More work than you can imagine goes into

00:07:05.608 --> 00:07:07.613
creating something that, in the end,

00:07:07.613 --> 00:07:09.930
might just be on screen
for a couple of seconds,

00:07:09.930 --> 00:07:12.200
but can still be the most memorable moment

00:07:12.200 --> 00:07:14.460
of any given movie or TV show.

00:07:14.460 --> 00:07:16.140
But it could be cool.

00:07:16.140 --> 00:07:17.720
If these would get
stained with berry juice,

00:07:17.720 --> 00:07:19.501
then it might actually
be kind of a cool look.

00:07:19.501 --> 00:07:20.334
Brian: Yeah.

00:07:20.334 --> 00:07:21.410
Ben: I don't know.

00:07:21.410 --> 00:07:22.710
The berries are delicious.

